\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Using Named Constants as Subscripts in Array Declarations\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab A named constant may be used as an array's highest subscript instead of a number\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab This is a common use for named constants\par
\pard\tx720\li720\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8211?\tab Highest subscript is often used multiple times\par
\pard\tx720\li720\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8211?\tab If highest subscript changes, use of a named constant allows it to be changed in one place\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 Const}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intMAX_SUBSCRIPT}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Integer}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf6 100}\line {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intArray}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 intMAX_SUBSCRIPT}{\cf5 )}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Integer}\par
