\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab An Array is a listof values \u8211? all values in an array are referenced by using the same name.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab An array is like a ListBox without the box where the list of values are all stored in memory.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Arrays are also called tables.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Arrays are based on the System.Array object \u8211? a type of collection object.  Arrays can store almost any type of data such as integer, string, decimal, etc.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Element \u8211? this is the term used to refer to an individual part of an array.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Index: 0 1 2 3 4 5\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Value: 2 4 6 8 10 12\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 11\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 One Dimension Array (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Each element is numbered beginning with the number zero.  Example, 0, 1, 2, 3, etc.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The number referring to an array elementis placed inside parentheses and is called a subscript or index.  This is the general format for an array reference.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab ArrayName(ElementNumber)\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Example references to array elements:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Element 4 of the array named AmountDecimal \u8211? remember that arrays start with 0 so the 4th element is numbered 3\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab AmountDecimal(3)\par
